Authorization models use "adding abstractions" to manage. 
Common abstraction models:

RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)
Users are assigned to roles (e.g., "HR Manager," "Sysadmin")
Roles are granted permissions, not users directly
Change someone's job? Just swap their role; no need to touch individual permission entries
Group-Based Access Control
Users are placed into groups (e.g., "Finance Team")
Permissions are assigned to the group
Common in Active Directory / Windows environments
ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control)
Access decisions are based on attributes (department, clearance level, time of day, device type)
More granular and dynamic than RBAC — policies can say "allow if department=Finance AND time=business hours"
Hierarchical / Inherited Permissions
Higher-level roles or groups pass down (inherit) permissions to lower ones
Reduces the need to redefine permissions at every level
Why This Matters
Scalability: adding a new employee means assigning a role, not writing custom rules
Auditability: easier to answer "who can access X?" by checking roles/groups instead of thousands of individual grants
Least privilege: roles can be scoped tightly, and users only get what their role needs
Consistency: reduces human error from manually setting permissions one-by-one

In short: abstraction means managing permissions at the level of roles, groups, or attributes rather than individual users — this is the foundation of enterprise IAM (Identity and Access Management) systems.
